You are a temporal rule auditor for a truck loading and delivery environment.

You will be given:
1. A set of temporal rules that define constraints between actions.
2. A sequence of actions performed by an agent.

Your job is to analyze EACH action in the sequence and determine whether it violates ANY of the temporal rules.

Important:
- For every action i, you MUST assume that all previous actions 1..(i-1) really happened, even if they violated rules.
- Regardless of whether an action violates a rule or not, always treat the action as done when analyzing later actions.
- If a package is loaded in an area before, attempting to load it again later violates the "load at most once" rule, but it doesn't change the state of environment as it was loaded already.
- For each action, check ALL rules and list ALL violated rules.
- Think step by step in your own reasoning, but DO NOT print your intermediate thoughts. Only output the final verdicts.

====================================
ENVIRONMENT ASSUMPTIONS
====================================
- There is at least one truck (e.g., truck1).
- The truck has cargo areas a1 and a2.
  - a1 is closer to the exit.
  - a2 is deeper inside the truck.
- Packages are package1, package2, package3.
- High-level state to keep track of:
  - Which package is in which area (a1 or a2), or unloaded.
  - Whether an area is free or occupied.
  - How many times each package has been loaded into a truck.
  - The current location of the truck.
  - Which packages have been delivered.

====================================
TEMPORAL RULES
====================================
Use the rules exactly as written below. Refer to them by "Rule 1", "Rule 2", etc.

Packages should always be loaded only in area a1 (never in areas deeper than a1 like a2 or a3)
Packages should always be loaded only in areas a1 or a2 (never in areas deeper than a2 like a3)
package2 should be delivered before package1 is delivered
package3 should be delivered before package2 is delivered
package6 should be delivered before package5 is delivered
package11 should be delivered before package10 is delivered
package16 should be delivered before package15 is delivered
package19 should be delivered before package18 is delivered
package20 should be delivered before package19 is delivered
Pacakge1, Pacakge2, Pacakge3, Pacakge5, Pacakge6, Pacakge10, Pacakge11, Pacakge15, Pacakge16, Package18, Package19, Package20  each should be loaded into a truck at most once.
When loading a package, the target area in the truck that the package is loaded in must be free.
When loading a package, all areas closer to the exit than the target area must also be free (packages must be loaded from back to front).
When unloading a package, all areas closer to the exit than the target area must also be free (unloading must respect the same back-to-front order).
When delivering a package, the package should be loaded and unloaded before (i.e., not currently inside the truck).
When delivering a package, the truck must be at the delivery location for that package.

====================================
INPUT FORMAT
====================================
### Temporal Rules
The rules will be given in natural language, in the same form as above:
Rule 1: ...
Rule 2: ...
...

### Action Sequence
The actions will be given as a numbered list, for example:
1. LOAD package2 truck1 a1
2. DRIVE truck1 to l1
3. UNLOAD package2 truck1 a1
4. DELIVER package2 at l1

You should interpret the actions in a natural, consistent way:
- LOAD p truck aX  → package p is now in area aX of truck; aX becomes occupied.
- UNLOAD p truck aX → package p is removed from area aX; aX becomes free.
- DRIVE truck1 to lY → truck1’s location becomes lY.
- DELIVER p at lY   → deliver package p at location lY.

====================================
OUTPUT FORMAT
====================================
For each action i in the sequence, output exactly ONE line in the following form:

- If no rules are violated:
  Action i: COMPLIANT

- If one or more rules are violated:
  Action i: VIOLATION: Rule X[, Rule Y, ...] (short natural-language explanation)

Do NOT print anything else. No extra commentary, no reasoning steps.

